===Motivation===

[[bip-0327.mediawiki|BIP327]] (MuSig2) allows a flat set of ''n'' co-signers to
create a single aggregate public key and cooperatively produce an ordinary
Schnorr signature that verifies under [[bip-0340.mediawiki|BIP340]]. The
aggregate public key is computed from the individual public keys of all
participants, and the resulting signature is indistinguishable from a signature
created by a single signer holding the corresponding aggregate secret key.

MuSig2 is an ''n''-of-''n'' multi-signature scheme in which every signer whose
public key is included in the aggregate key must participate in each signing
session. After the aggregate key has been established, signers exchange public
nonces and then partial signatures, allowing signing to complete in two
communication rounds.

This model works well when the signing group is naturally flat: each participant
is a single logical signer, and every participant is expected to contribute to
every signature. However, many Bitcoin applications are not naturally flat. A
participant in one protocol may itself be a signing group with its own internal
MuSig2 aggregate key, coordination process, and availability assumptions.
Flattening all such internal keys into a single MuSig2 aggregate removes this
group structure and treats every leaf key as a direct participant in one global
signing session.

In such applications, flattening can have several undesirable effects. It can
reveal internal signer sets to other protocol participants, require parties
outside a group to reason about that group's internal key structure, and make
the outer protocol depend directly on every individual leaf signer rather than
on logical signer groups.

Consider the following examples:

* '''Institutional custody''': a custodian may operate several geographically separated signing devices under an internal MuSig2 policy. Externally, the custodian may appear as one logical signer in a protocol shared with a client or another institution. Flattening the custodian's internal keys together with the external participant's keys exposes the custodian's internal signer set to the counterparty and makes the external protocol depend directly on every internal device.

* '''Lightning channel factories''': a channel factory may involve participants that are themselves represented by MuSig2 signing groups. Flattening every underlying key into one aggregate key removes the distinction between participants and their internal signing groups. It also turns signing into a single all-leaf-signers-required protocol rather than a protocol between logical participants.

* '''Ark-style VTXO trees''': Ark and related constructions organize virtual UTXOs using trees of off-chain transactions. When aggregate keys are used at multiple levels of such a construction, a flat list of all leaf keys does not naturally preserve the hierarchical relationship between transaction-tree nodes and signing groups.

* '''Corporate treasury''': an executive group, a compliance group, and a finance group may each maintain separate MuSig2 signing arrangements. A final treasury authorization may require participation by each group while keeping each group's internal signer set hidden from the others. Flattening all keys into one aggregate exposes those internal structures and makes the protocol depend on every individual leaf signer.

NestedMuSig2 addresses this by allowing a participant public key at one level of
a MuSig2 signing session to itself be a MuSig2 aggregate key. The result is a
tree of signing groups whose root key is still an ordinary BIP340-compatible
x-only public key, and whose final signature is still an ordinary BIP340
signature.

This preserves the verifier-facing properties of MuSig2. A verifier only sees a
BIP340 public key and a BIP340 signature. The verifier does not need to know
whether the signing key was produced by a single signer, a flat MuSig2 group, or
a nested tree of MuSig2 groups.

NestedMuSig2 also preserves the two-round signing structure of MuSig2. Each leaf
signer participates in one nonce-exchange round and one partial-signature round,
while aggregation is performed recursively by coordinators at each level of the
tree.

This BIP specifies the additional nonce aggregation, signing, and partial
signature aggregation rules required to make such recursive composition
well-defined and compatible with BIP340 verification.

==Algorithms==

The following notation and all primitive algorithms (''IndividualPubkey'',
''KeySort'', ''KeyAgg'', ''KeyAggCoeff'', ''ApplyTweak'', ''GetXonlyPubkey'',
''GetPlainPubkey'', ''NonceGen'', ''NonceAgg'') are as defined in
[[bip-0327.mediawiki|BIP327]]. This BIP does not restate them; implementers must
implement BIP327 first.

===Notation===

All notation from BIP327 §Algorithms' §Notation applies here. Additional
notation introduced by this BIP:

; Tree structure

* ''&Lambda;'' denotes the depth of the signing tree, a positive integer. Depth ''0'' is the root; depth ''&Lambda;−1'' is the leaf level.
* ''d'' denotes a tree depth index, ranging from ''0'' (root) to ''&Lambda;−1'' (leaf).
* ''n<sub>d</sub>'' denotes the number of participants (keys or sub-groups) at depth ''d''.
* ''pk<sub>i,d</sub>'' denotes participant ''i'' 's plain public key at depth ''d''. At depth &Lambda;−1 this is an individual signer's plain public key. At depths 0..&Lambda;−2 it is the plain aggregate key of a sub-tree, i.e. ''cbytes(KeyAgg(L<sub>d</sub>).Q)'' for the appropriate child list.
* ''L<sub>d</sub>'' denotes the ordered list of plain public keys that are input to ''KeyAgg'' at depth ''d''. The list ''L<sub>0</sub>'' produces the root aggregate key ''X&#771;''.

; Nonces

* ''r<sub>1,j</sub>'' for ''j = 1..ν'' denotes signer 1's secret nonce scalars, sampled uniformly at random. These appear in the signing equation and must never be reused. 
* ''out<sup>d</sup>'' denotes the 66-byte aggregate nonce at depth ''d'', produced by running ''NonceAgg'' then ''NonceAggExt'' at that level. ''out<sup>0</sup>'' is the root aggregate nonce. ''out<sup>d</sup>'' for ''d ≥ 1'' is the aggregate nonce of the sub-tree at depth ''d'', as passed up to the parent.
* ''(R<sub>1,0</sub>, ..., R<sub>ν,0</sub>) := out<sup>0</sup>'' denotes the ν nonce points extracted from the root aggregate nonce.

; Per-level key-aggregation coefficients

* ''a<sub>1,d</sub>'' denotes signer 1's key-aggregation coefficient at depth ''d'', computed as ''KeyAggCoef(L<sub>d</sub>, pk<sub>1,d</sub>)''. One coefficient exists per level the signer traverses from leaf to root.

; Per-level nonce binding factors

* ''b<sub>d+1</sub>'' for ''d = &Lambda;−2, ..., 0'' denotes the nonce binding factor derived at depth ''d'' during ''Sign<nowiki>'</nowiki>'', computed as ''H<sub>non</sub>(pk<sub>1,d</sub>, out<sup>d+1</sup>)''. It binds the sub-tree's aggregate nonce to signer 1's aggregate key at that level. Uses the <code>NestedMuSig/aggnoncecoef</code> tag. No message is included.
* ''b<sub>0</sub>'' denotes the root-level nonce binding factor, computed as ''H<sub>non&#773;</sub>(X&#771;, (R<sub>1,0</sub>, ..., R<sub>ν,0</sub>), m)''. It binds the root aggregate nonce to the root aggregate key and the message. Uses the <code>NestedMuSig/noncecoef</code> tag. The message is included.

; Accumulated products (computed in ''Sign<nowiki>'</nowiki>'')

* ''b&#780;'' (b-check) denotes the accumulated product of all per-level nonce binding factors: ''b&#780; := &#8719;<sub>ℓ=0</sub><sup>&Lambda;−1</sup> b<sub>ℓ</sub> mod p''.
* ''&#269;'' (c-check) denotes the accumulated challenge coefficient: ''&#269; := c · &#8719;<sub>ℓ=0</sub><sup>&Lambda;−1</sup> a<sub>1,ℓ</sub> mod p'', where ''c := H<sub>sig</sub>(X&#771;, R, m)''.

; Effective root nonce

* ''R'' denotes the effective root nonce point, computed from the root aggregate nonce as ''R := &#8719;<sub>j=1</sub><sup>ν</sup> R<sub>j,0</sub><sup>b<sub>0</sub><sup>j−1</sup></sup>''. For ν = 2 this is ''R<sub>1,0</sub> · R<sub>2,0</sub><sup>b<sub>0</sub></sup>''.

; Final signing equation (from ''Sign<nowiki>'</nowiki>'')

The partial signature produced by signer 1 is:
: ''s<sub>1</sub> := č · sk<sub>1</sub> + Σ<sub>j=1</sub><sup>ν</sup> r<sub>1,j</sub> · b&#780;<sup>j−1</sup> mod p''

For ν = 2 this reduces to ''s<sub>1</sub> = č · sk<sub>1</sub> + r<sub>1,1</sub> + r<sub>1,2</sub> · b&#780; mod p''.

===Tagged Hashes===

This BIP introduces the following tagged hash functions. All tagged hashes use the BIP340 tagged-hash construction:

: ''SHA256(SHA256(tag) || SHA256(tag) || x)''

where ''tag'' is a UTF-8 encoded string. The tag strings used by this BIP are distinct from the tag strings used by BIP327 to provide domain separation between MuSig2 and NestedMuSig2 sessions.

{| class="wikitable"
|-
! Tag string !! Used in
|-
| <code>NestedMuSig/aux</code> || ''NonceGen'', ''DeterministicSign''
|-
| <code>NestedMuSig/nonce</code> || ''NonceGen''
|-
| <code>NestedMuSig/aggnoncecoef</code> || ''NonceAggExt''
|-
| <code>NestedMuSig/noncecoef</code> || ''GetSessionValues''
|-
| <code>NestedMuSig/deterministic/nonce</code> || ''DeterministicSign''
|}

The tags <code>NestedMuSig/aggnoncecoef</code> and <code>NestedMuSig/noncecoef</code> correspond to distinct random-oracle roles in the security proof (''H<sub>non</sub>'' and ''H<sub>non&#773;</sub>'' respectively in [[https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/223|Kohen (2026)]] and must use separate tags. Using a single shared tag for both would violate the domain-separation requirement of the security reduction.

The Schnorr challenge MUST use the existing <code>BIP0340/challenge</code> tag and MUST NOT use a NestedMuSig-specific tag. This ensures the final signature is an ordinary BIP340 signature that passes standard BIP340 verification without modification.
